why 1 0 RANGE The bass starts high on D and walks down D–C–B♭: a light opening that descends through the phrase.
why 3 5/8 LEAPS After its leap down the tenor keeps falling A–G–F before turning, reaching the low F of the coming chord by step.
why 5 1/2 DIRECT The tune leaps up a fourth to D while the bass steps up F♯–G: a hidden fifth softened by the step in the bass. The leap belongs to the hymn tune.
why 6 0 SIMILAR_MOTION Everything rises out of V of V: the bass's G♯ resolves to A, the tenor's B to C♯, and the tune climbs to F. Each line rises for its own reason.
why 6 1/4 DOUBLING The tenor's C♯ rises to D as a leading tone should, while the tune falls a third to D. Both land on D.
why 7 1/4 CROSS_RELATION The alto's E, then E♭ in the tenor: the music turns from A minor toward G minor. The E♭ is a leaning note in the tenor's line D–E♭–D, so the ear follows it as part of that line.
why 8 0 CROSSING The tune holds G while the alto climbs A–B♭–C above it, so for a beat the alto is on top. Bach lets the inner line rise while the melody waits.
why 8 1/8 CROSSING The alto stays above the soprano's held G.
why 8 1/4 CROSSING The alto reaches C, its high point, still above the held soprano.
why 8 1/4 SPACING The alto climbs while the tenor falls B♭–A–G, so the space between them opens for a beat.
why 8 3/8 CROSSING The last eighth of the crossing; at the fermata the soprano moves up to A and is on top again.
why 8 3/8 SPACING The widest point; at the fermata the alto falls to F♯ and the gap closes.
why 8 1/2 SEVENTH The tenor's D, the seventh of the E♭ chord, falls away through B♭–A–G and returns to D for the fermata, where D is the root of the D major chord.
why 9 0 RANGE The new phrase starts with the bass high on D, walking down D–C–B–B♭–A. Starting high gives it room to descend.
